{
  "extends": "@react-native/typescript-config",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "types": ["jest"],
    // The third of the three declarations — see metro.config.js. `baseUrl` is
    // required for `paths` to mean anything.
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": {
      "@cartze/core": ["../core/src/index.ts"],
      "@cartze/core/*": ["../core/src/*"],
      // The compiler's half of Metro's node_modules proxy. A file under
      // ../core that imports `react-native` resolves upward from its OWN
      // folder and never reaches this app's copy — tsc reports it as
      // "Cannot find module 'react-native'" while the bundler is perfectly
      // happy, which is the two-out-of-three failure the comment in
      // metro.config.js warns about, caught here on the first slice.
      // `react` is mapped at its TYPES first and the package second, and the
      // order is the whole trick. Pointing it straight at `./node_modules/react`
      // resolves to a JS entry with no declarations, TypeScript does not then
      // fall back to `@types/react`, and every `import React` in the app
      // silently becomes `any` with JSX losing its prop checking. Measured on
      // the first slice; the guard asserts the pair rather than the absence
      // it used to assert.
      "react": ["./node_modules/@types/react", "./node_modules/react"],
      // react-native ships its own types, so one entry is enough.
      "react-native": ["./node_modules/react-native"],
       // EVERYTHING ELSE, and this is the entry that scales.
       //
       // A file under ../core importing `react-native-svg`, `react-navigation`
       // or `react-native-safe-area-context` resolves upward from its OWN
       // folder and reaches none of them. Adding a `paths` line per package
       // would mean this file needs editing every time core touches a new
       // dependency — a rule that has to be remembered is a rule that gets
       // forgotten.
       //
       // The wildcard is LAST, so the explicit entries above still win. For
       // files inside this app it changes nothing: `./node_modules/*` is where
       // node would have looked anyway.
       //
       // `@types` comes FIRST inside it, and that ordering is the same lesson
       // as the `react` entry above, learned twice: put the package first and
       // a library whose types live in a separate @types package resolves to
       // a JS entry with no declarations. TypeScript does not fall back — it
       // reports implicit-any, and `react-test-renderer` took the whole test
       // tree down that way. A package that ships its OWN types has no @types
       // directory, so it falls through to the second entry untouched.
      "*": ["./node_modules/@types/*", "./node_modules/*"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"],
  "exclude": ["**/node_modules", "**/Pods"]
}
